Lab Setup
Verify systemd version:
systemctl --version
Check existing timers:
systemctl list-timers --all
Task 1: Create and Start a Systemd Timer for System Maintenance
Subtask 1.1: Create a Service Unit
Create a service unit file:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/maintenance.service
Add the following content:
[Unit]
Description=System Maintenance Task

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/echo "Performing system maintenance at $(date)" >> /var/log/maintenance.log
Save and exit the editor
Subtask 1.2: Create a Timer Unit
Create the timer unit file:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/maintenance.timer
Add this configuration:
[Unit]
Description=Run maintenance daily

[Timer]
OnCalendar=daily
Persistent=true
Unit=maintenance.service

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
Subtask 1.3: Enable and Start the Timer
Reload systemd:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
Enable and start the timer:
sudo systemctl enable --now maintenance.timer
Verify the status:
systemctl status maintenance.timer
Expected Outcome: Timer is active and will trigger daily. Check /var/log/maintenance.log after 24 hours.

Task 2: Learn How to Create Timer Units
Subtask 2.1: Understand Timer Specifications
Key timer directives:

OnCalendar: Calendar event expressions
OnActiveSec: Relative to timer activation
OnBootSec: Relative to system boot
OnUnitActiveSec: Relative to last activation
Subtask 2.2: Create a Complex Timer
Create a new timer:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer
Add this configuration:
[Unit]
Description=Weekly backup with specific time

[Timer]
OnCalendar=Mon *-*-* 02:00:00
Unit=backup.service
Persistent=true

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
Troubleshooting Tip: Test calendar expressions with:

systemd-analyze calendar "Mon *-*-* 02:00:00"
Task 3: Modify Systemd Timer Settings
Subtask 3.1: Adjust Existing Timer
Edit the maintenance timer:
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/maintenance.timer
Modify to run hourly:
OnCalendar=hourly
Subtask 3.2: Add Randomized Delay
Add this to prevent system load spikes:
RandomizedDelaySec=5m
Subtask 3.3: Verify Changes
Reload and check next execution:
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl list-timers
Monitoring and Troubleshooting
Check Timer Execution
journalctl -u maintenance.service
Verify Timer Accuracy
systemd-analyze calendar --iterations=5 "hourly"
Common Issues
Timer not triggering? Check:
systemctl status <timer>.timer
journalctl -xe
Service not starting? Verify:
systemctl status <service>.service
